WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.722 --> 00:00:05.930
RAVI LACHHMAN: Hi, everybody.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:07.180
My name is Ravi Lachhman.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.120
I'm an evangelist at Harness
and very excited to be

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.190
talking at Google Cloud Next.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.810
I had a lot of
outages in my day,

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:15.760
so hopefully we can
learn a little bit

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:17.060
how not to get burned.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:20.110
But also with me is my
esteemed colleague, Brett.

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:21.798
Brett?

00:00:21.798 --> 00:00:23.590
BRETT ZANE-ULMAN: Hi,
I'm Brett Zane-Ulman.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.320
I'm an engineering
manager here at Harness.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.390
And I'm excited
to be here today.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:30.410
Go ahead, Ravi.

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:31.470
RAVI LACHHMAN: Perfect.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.630
So what we're going to
be talking about today--

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.330
so at Harness,
we've actually went

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:37.573
through a pretty big
migration on GCP.

00:00:37.573 --> 00:00:38.990
But a little bit
of level setting,

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.090
what on earth is Harness?

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:44.570
And we'd like to decide
our time before GCP,

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.430
our time on the way to
GCP, and our time on GCP.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.080
It's very nicely segmented here.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.110
So in the beginning, let's talk
a little bit about Harness.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.030
A divine light came
down and shined

00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:00.920
upon the continuous delivery
world, and Harness was born.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.930
So again, a bright light shined.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:05.693
So what are we actually
talking about here?

00:01:05.693 --> 00:01:06.860
We're talking about Harness.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.930
At Harness, we're a continuous
delivery as a service.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.280
So what does that mean?

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.130
A continuous
delivery as a service

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.050
is something that
helps orchestrate

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:16.160
a couple of parts for you.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.490
So getting your software or
your platforms into production

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.220
really requires orchestration
between approvals,

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:23.870
all the confidence
building steps

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.810
that you have, such as
testing, and also just a way

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:27.890
to do release, right?

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.310
So are you leveraging a safe
type of release like a canary?

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.330
So you see the canary
doing a dab there.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.685
Are you doing a blue-green?

00:01:34.685 --> 00:01:37.040
Now if these terms are
new to you, don't worry.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.115
We'll be here after the
presentation to kind of answer

00:01:40.115 --> 00:01:40.820
some questions.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.830
But Harness really packages all
that up for you as a service.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.620
Also, since we have a lot of
customers that depend on us,

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.140
we always have to be up.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.750
And so what the Harness
platform does is exactly that.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:58.360
We're supporting over 100
enterprises, thousands

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.930
of community users, and
also, hundreds of thousands

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:03.370
of deployments regularly.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.680
Ironically, we actually
help empower people coming

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.110
and talking on stages
in different technology

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:09.970
conferences because
if you're looking

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.475
at where their applications
go, most likely,

00:02:12.475 --> 00:02:14.350
they could be using
Harness in the background

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.220
to help deploy that.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.170
And so what do we
deal with at Harness?

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.480
We actually have some unique
challenges at Harness,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.530
is that we have a SaaS
version of our product.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.050
So people expect
uptime all the time.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.740
But also, we're supporting two
other type of cloud deployment

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:30.370
models.

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.230
I like to call it
kind of SaaS, right?

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.660
So certain customers
might not like being

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.903
completely in the public cloud.

00:02:36.903 --> 00:02:38.320
And so there's
limited connection.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.195
They're running parts
of the Harness platform

00:02:40.195 --> 00:02:41.380
on their hardware.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:44.050
And also, we have a
completely Air Gap version--

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:46.060
I call it No SaaS'in Around--

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.210
of the products so customers
can be completely disconnected,

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.790
again, in trying to leverage
the Harness platform.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.340
And so what are some of the
challenges of the platform,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:55.840
right?

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.257
Because again, we
have to maintain

00:02:57.257 --> 00:02:59.270
three different versions
of our platform.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:01.150
So the first thing
before our time with GCP

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.120
was scaling of the platform
is basically non-existent.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.290
We had a very
rigid architecture.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.950
And Brett will walk through
that in a couple of minutes.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:11.650
Also, this was our first
foray into Kubernetes.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.200
Actually, none of our
workload for ourselves

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.630
were hosted or deployed
on the Kubernetes.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:19.150
Now a lot of our customers
are using Kubernetes.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.670
But it was actually our
first foray into Kubernetes.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:23.200
We went to GCP.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.870
And also, we had no
separation of duty, right?

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:28.090
So what that means
is that we didn't

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:29.650
have lower
environments deploying

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:31.233
to other different
lower environments.

00:03:31.233 --> 00:03:33.070
Basically, our prod
environments that we

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.620
were building the platform
on deployed straight to prod.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.110
So we had the lack of
separation of duties.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:39.730
And also, we had
multiple versions

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:43.090
of the platform, which was
becoming difficult to maintain.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:45.640
And also, we were
completely on SaaS.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.520
So that means that any
sort of downtime for us

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.830
would be very detrimental
to the customers.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.250
And so, I'm about to
hand it over to Brett.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:58.280
Let's talk about our time,
Brett, before the mighty GCP.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:03:59.540
Brett?

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.950
BRETT ZANE-ULMAN:
Sure, Ravi, thanks.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.620
So as Ravi mentioned, our
production environment

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.300
prior to moving to
GCP was pretty rigid.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.550
This is basically
what it looked like.

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.870
It's very simple.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.310
We had just a couple
of EC2 instances that

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:23.020
were hosting our UI, which
is just some static content

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:27.280
and then three other VMs running
our orchestration servers

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.770
and connecting to our database.

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:31.870
Each one was behind
a load balancer.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:33.520
And that was pretty much it.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.510
What we had to do is
update every time we

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.250
want to deploy a new version.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.440
And Harness is a
deployment tool.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.130
So of course, we're using
Harness to deploy Harness.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:48.670
But at this time, we didn't have
any other environments set up

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.330
for deploying to production.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:53.620
So production deployed itself.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.330
And how did this work?

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.140
So our servers, which
are called managers,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.720
orchestrate workflow
executions during a deployment.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:05.830
But the actual calls to
deploy and update a service

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:09.070
are done by a worker, or
delegate, in the customer

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.640
environment.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.600
So in this case, being
our own customer,

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.910
our delegate is in the
same VPC as the servers.

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:18.730
And while the
workflow runs, it goes

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:21.550
through one server at a
time, updating the binary

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:23.360
and restarting the service.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:26.110
This is all well and good,
except that during the course

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:28.000
of the deployment, the
orchestration engine

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.670
itself gets updated
to a new version.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:33.160
And since it relies on
communicating back and forth

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.820
with the delegate, it's very
sensitive to data model changes

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.580
that may have been introduced
by the new version.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:42.130
Of course, if this
goes wrong and hangs,

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:43.810
then you get stuck
with production

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.160
in an intermediate state.

00:05:48.235 --> 00:05:50.610
RAVI LACHHMAN: And now it's
time to introduce Kubernetes.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.350
So given all the
challenges that we had,

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:55.530
looking to really leverage
Kubernetes to help

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.240
overcome that, a
lot of our customers

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.010
are moving towards Kubernetes.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.920
And also our platform actually
deploys to Kubernetes.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.130
So the next rational
step for us was actually

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:07.060
to be going towards Kubernetes.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:09.120
Brett, let's talk
about some reasons

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.012
why we were looking to leverage
our very own Kubernetes.

00:06:13.012 --> 00:06:14.220
BRETT ZANE-ULMAN: Sure, Ravi.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:18.270
So, as Ravi mentioned, over
the first year that we even

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:22.080
had a production environment,
we saw the rise of Kubernetes

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.850
in popularity.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.250
It was becoming more and more
popular with our customers.

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:30.940
And so we wanted to
leverage that for ourselves.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:33.610
Our primary goal was to
dockerize our microservices

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.420
and start using Kubernetes.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.750
And when we looked
around at the time,

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:41.770
GKE was really the best and one
of the only hosted Kubernetes

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:43.120
services.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.650
We already had Kubernetes
support in Harness on GKE,

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.810
so why not leverage that
and use it for ourselves?

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.560
So we built out
the infrastructure

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:55.780
and defined all of our
Kubernetes configuration

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:59.270
in Harness to deploy all
of our microservices,

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:03.370
exposing an NGINX Ingress
Controller via a load balancer

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.740
and then routing to
our various services.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.130
So once this had been
working in QA for some time,

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:13.870
we were ready to make
the switch in production.

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:17.960
So making the migration
is what we're here for.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.510
So let's talk about that.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:21.760
RAVI LACHHMAN: Yeah, this
is what the people came for.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:23.653
We have two pieces of
infrastructure now.

00:07:23.653 --> 00:07:24.820
How do we make the solution?

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.880
[INAUDIBLE] And it's
so kind of gearing up

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:31.360
towards the switch, kind of the
ethos that we have at Harness,

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:33.520
that we help enable
people doing production

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:35.710
changes in normal hours, right?

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.697
If you kind of take a look
back at years gone by,

00:07:38.697 --> 00:07:40.780
usually, you're deploying
during your green window

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:41.500
or a black window.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.458
Pick your color, right?

00:07:42.458 --> 00:07:44.410
It's 2:00 or 3:00
in the morning.

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:45.940
And everyone's tired, right?

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:48.100
And so since we
help enable people

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:51.220
make this type of deployment
or really critical deployments

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:53.920
in normal business
hours for the geography,

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:56.260
we decided to drink
our own champagne

00:07:56.260 --> 00:07:57.790
or eat our own dog
food, that, hey,

00:07:57.790 --> 00:07:59.675
let's do a dinner time
switch, because we

00:07:59.675 --> 00:08:00.550
had two sets of team.

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:02.940
So around dinnertime
in San Francisco

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:04.490
is actually breakfast
time in India.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:07.940
So for all the resources
that we had to line up,

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.620
it made the best sense for us
to do it around dinner time.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:13.570
And so Brett, let's talk a
little bit about the switch

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:14.860
that we had to do.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:15.940
BRETT ZANE-ULMAN: Sure.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:19.700
So we were migrating not
only our own infrastructure,

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:23.550
but also our database, which
is a hosted Atlas Mongo DB

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:25.850
cluster.

00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:28.420
While the old production
instances on the left

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:32.470
were still live, we deployed and
brought up the new Kubernetes

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:35.620
stack, pointed at the
database that was still

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:39.340
in AWS at the time, just to
make sure it was running fine,

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:41.789
well connected to
the production DB,

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.390
but without any traffic
reaching it yet.

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:48.430
So once that works, we
scaled it back down.

00:08:48.430 --> 00:08:52.540
Then, for the database, we
live migrated the data over

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:57.420
to another Atlas cluster,
this time hosted in GCP.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.760
The data there was keeping
pace with the live production

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.770
database with a
few seconds of lag.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:07.000
Then, to make the switch, we
took a few minutes of downtime,

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.550
turned down the old
production instances,

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:12.500
so that they stop
writing to the database,

00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:15.250
then wait a few moments for
the database live migration

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:17.110
to catch up completely.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:20.950
Then we finalized
the data migration.

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:23.900
So then we reconfigured the
new Kubernetes manager to point

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:28.040
to the new database in GCP
and redeployed with Harness.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.020
So once that was up and
verified on our end,

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:34.280
the only thing left was
to switch the DNS entry

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:38.462
to the IP of the
new load balancer.

00:09:38.462 --> 00:09:39.420
RAVI LACHHMAN: Awesome.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:42.390
And not wanting to
do the big reveal,

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:44.315
but it's in the title
of our presentation.

00:09:44.315 --> 00:09:45.690
How long do you
think it actually

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:49.194
took us to make the kind
of [INAUDIBLE] switch?

00:09:49.194 --> 00:09:51.650
And the big reveal--
this is actually

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:54.320
live Slack from when
we made the switch.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.300
It was a total of 76 minutes.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:01.410
And it was very impressive
that this change only took

00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.200
76 minutes for us to execute.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:05.700
But maybe we could
talk a little bit

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.740
about what the final ecosystem
looked like for us, given

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:11.490
the benefits and some
of the lacking stuff

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:13.592
we had in our old
architecture, Brett.

00:10:13.592 --> 00:10:14.550
BRETT ZANE-ULMAN: Sure.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.010
So I mentioned previously
some of the problems

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:20.040
with the self deployment
model and the data

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.240
model changes that
can cause problems

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.320
when we upgrade ourselves.

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:28.440
So what we've done
now, we've added

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:30.120
a number of new microservices.

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:33.510
And we've introduced a new
internal Harness environment

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:35.880
that deploys production,
so we're not self

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:37.710
deploying anymore.

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:42.060
And we've also introduced
versioning to address the data

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:44.050
model changes that I mentioned.

00:10:44.050 --> 00:10:47.820
So the payload is synchronized
between the servers

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:51.630
and the workers at all
times via the versioning.

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:55.350
So now we're able to
quickly scale using GKE

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:59.280
and meet new demand or deal
with production issues.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:06.610
This is an example of
what our pipelines look

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:08.300
like while they're running.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:12.370
And with the products using
these types of pipelines,

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:15.190
we're able to wrangle all
the configuration that you

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:18.010
need to stand up your services.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:19.750
And that definitely
helped us when

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:22.900
we were changing both the cloud
provider and the deployment

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:24.907
model at the same time.

00:11:24.907 --> 00:11:26.240
RAVI LACHHMAN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.615
And I think just kind of adding
to some of Brett's points

00:11:28.615 --> 00:11:32.690
there, we are a continuous
delivery company.

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:34.690
And it really shows
the importance

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:36.740
for making such a big
infrastructure change that

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:38.310
actually leverage a pipeline.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:40.720
As we were going through
the development and also

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:43.690
the testing of this,
it's kind of interesting,

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.660
as the kind of the shift of
infrastructure to the cloud

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:49.960
allows for a lot more iteration,
and so running the pipelines,

00:11:49.960 --> 00:11:53.650
kind of testing out what would
happen in success scenarios,

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:56.963
what would happen in failure
scenarios, was quite important.

00:11:56.963 --> 00:11:58.630
So it wasn't all,
this is the first time

00:11:58.630 --> 00:11:59.590
we're doing something.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:03.220
There was a lot of trial
and error to get this right.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:06.100
But with all of that, with all
the trials and tribulations,

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.660
right, we made a
fairly big change.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:10.890
And it has really
paid off for us,

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:13.480
partnering with Google Cloud,
that we're able to, A, we're

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:15.580
increasing the number of
customers that we have,

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:17.890
the infrastructure
scaling appropriately,

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:20.870
and just really allowing us to
grow and scale our business.

00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:22.880
And so don't forget
after all this is done

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:25.930
and to celebrate success,
and really looking

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:28.990
at a very simple equation
for what is success, right?

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:31.120
So there was certainly a
lot of prep work, right,

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:33.640
so gathering and
planning, taking inventory

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:36.915
of what you have, making sure
that these particular pieces,

00:12:36.915 --> 00:12:39.415
especially as we started to
move towards more dockerization,

00:12:39.415 --> 00:12:39.915
right?

00:12:39.915 --> 00:12:42.940
It's a very, very [INAUDIBLE]
approach that we took.

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:44.860
Also, for execution,
make sure you

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:46.130
have the right staff in place.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:47.290
If it's 2:00 or
3:00 in the morning,

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:49.390
it doesn't do anybody
a favor, right, if it's

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:51.310
a time that everyone's awake.

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:53.090
You're certainly more alert.

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.240
And also, the journey
is never over, right?

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:58.270
A common thing that we like to
ask our prospects and customers

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:00.400
is, hey, when is
your deployment over?

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:01.897
It's kind of an
intrinsic question.

00:13:01.897 --> 00:13:03.730
Because, again, you
always want to make sure

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:06.250
that you're always refining
and continually approving

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:07.740
what you do have.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:11.710
That kind of lasting impact that
we have for Google Cloud, even

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:14.250
our leveraging
technologies like GKE,

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:18.940
when we have new engineers even
from field sales to customer

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:22.990
support to even engineering
where Brett's at, teaching them

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:25.610
on Kubernetes and GKE makes
it easier for us, right?

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:27.610
So they have an easy way
to kind of see and play

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:28.940
with Kubernetes.

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:31.360
And thanks once again for
catching our presentation.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.850
Brett and I really appreciated
you coming to catch our talk.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:36.743
And hopefully, we can
catch up with everybody

00:13:36.743 --> 00:13:37.910
